{
  "layer": "solution",
  "type": "component",
  "name": "template-registry",
  "body": "Template registry / schema engine: schema model (fields, types, formats, body rules, sections) populated from project `template.yaml` manifests by the template-library-loader, with each template's kind taken from its directory (slides/ or sections/). Load-time checks (cycles rejected with the chain in the error — a reference is a declared `sections:` acceptance, a `section` helper call in a layout, or a `child` constructor's target, section-helper-load-checks, child-constructor —, composition depth no greater than six heading levels, directory-kind check for section acceptance and field types, cross-kind name uniqueness, reserved names — `notes`, `body`, the `_format` suffix and the reserved context names `deck`/`slide`/`item`/`raw`/`data` —, references, and recursive example validation — each example layout executed against the merged layout namespace built from every library layout text with the template's own layer function map, so `{{ template \"<name>\" . }}` composition resolves per layer while `media` binds to the template's own layer (example-layout-composition) —), including the static `section` helper checks (the target exists and is section-usage, literal `dict` keys are declared fields, required fields present or defaulted, each literal child argument's template is accepted by the target's declared sections and its fields/body/nested children validate, the literal child set satisfies each declaration's min/max), enforcement that no template, theme or media name is carried by both library layers (library-layer-uniqueness), resolution of a local template's `section` helper call against the merged template set (library-layer-references), lookup and description. Owns the central, documented layout function map (`media \"path\"` → served URL of the file at its layer's `media/<path>`; the path is relative to that layer's `media/` and stays within it; a theme's files are addressed from its theme directory, by a relative path or the reserved `theme:<name>/` prefix, never through `media`; plus the `section`, `dict`, `list`, `child` and `children` helpers — template-language, section-helper, dict-list-helpers, child-constructor, children-forwarding). Templates come solely from the resolved library layers (global.constraint.no-embedded-template-assets).",
